## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for privacy-preserving, real-time NLP at the edge in a multilingual enterprise. It emphasizes latency, data governance, explainability, and robust rollout/rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Edge + cloud hybrid architecture with privacy budgets (differential privacy or federated learning)
- Lightweight multilingual encoders (distilled models) for on-device inference and PII redaction
- Streaming governance registry for compliance audit trails
- Drift detection on model outputs and safe rollback via versioned model artifacts

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactPII(text) {
  return text
    .replace(/[a-z0-9._%+-]+@[a-z0-9.-]+\.[a-z]{2,}/gi, '[EMAIL_REDACTED]')
    .replace(/\b\d{3}[-.]?\d{3}[-.]?\d{4}\b/g, '[PHONE_REDACTED]')
    .replace(/\b\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}\b/g, '[SSN_REDACTED]');
}

async function processMessage(message, edgeModel) {
  const redacted = redactPII(message.content);
  const prediction = await edgeModel.classify(redacted);

  if (prediction.confidence > 0.9) {
    await registry.log({ 
      violation: prediction.label, 
      redactedContent: redacted,
      timestamp: Date.now() 
    });
  }
  return { flagged: prediction.confidence > 0.9, prediction };
}
```

## Stack Recommendation
- **Edge**: TensorFlow Lite / ONNX Runtime Mobile, distilled XLM-R or mBERT
- **Cloud**: Python backend (FastAPI), Docker/Kubernetes, Redis for streaming
- **Monitoring**: Prometheus + Grafana, MLflow for model versioning
- **Metrics**: P99 latency, precision/recall per language, drift score (KL divergence), privacy budget consumption

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to architect a production-grade, multimodal NLP system that balances real-time performance with privacy requirements while supporting multilingual deployment.

## Key Concepts
- Multimodal fusion strategies and on-device inference
- Privacy-preserving cloud inference with encrypted fallback
- Latency budgeting and threshold calibration across languages
- Model explainability and fairness/drift monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function fuse(textModel, visualModel, audioModel) {
  // Attention-weighted fusion of modality scores
  const textScore = textModel.predict(textInput);
  const visualScore = visualModel.predict(videoFrames);
  const audioScore = audioModel.predict(audioStream);

  // Attention-based calibration head
  const weights = attentionLayer([textScore, visualScore, audioScore]);
  return weightedSum([textScore, visualScore, audioScore], weights);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design privacy-conscious NLP systems that run entirely in-browser, handling three languages with minimal computational resources. It emphasizes practical hybrid approaches (rule-based + neural) and on-device labeling loops for continuous improvement without data leaving the user's device.

## Key Concepts
- In-browser NLP with lightweight, privacy-preserving models
- Hybrid architecture: rule-based systems combined with small neural networks
- Multilingual tokenization and cross-language adaptation
- Privacy-preserving data collection through on-device labeling
- Drift monitoring with held-out evaluation data and user feedback loops

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal in-browser classifier skeleton
function predict(text) {
  const t = text.toLowerCase();
  let score = 0;

  // Rule-based sentiment cues
  const positiveWords = ['awesome', 'great', 'excellent', 'good', 'amazing'];
  const negativeWords = ['bad', 'terrible', 'awful', 'poor', 'worst'];

  positiveWords.forEach(w => { if (t.includes(w)) score += 1; });
  negativeWords.forEach(w => { if (t.includes(w)) score -= 1; });

  // Determine sentiment
  if (score > 0) return 'positive';
  if (score < 0) return 'negative';
  return 'neutral';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests end-to-end NLP system design with real-world constraints including fast latency, multilingual support, and explainability in a developer documentation context.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid retrieval combining dense (neural) and lexical (BM25) approaches
- Intent and slot extraction for query understanding
- Latency targets with p95 guarantees (<150ms)
- Privacy-preserving query handling via hashing
- Explainability through token contribution scoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractIntentAndSlots(query) {
  const intents = ['info', 'api', 'example'];
  const slots = ['language', 'framework', 'version'];
  // Lightweight classifier implementation
  return { intent: classify(query, intents), slots: extract(query, slots) };
}

async function hybridRetrieve(query, k=10) {
  const denseResults = await denseSearch(query, k);
  const bm25Results = await bm25Search(query, k);
  return fuseResults(denseResults, bm25Results);
}
```

